From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Anderson <thomasanderson@chromium.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 14:00:39 -0700
Subject: Reland "Require a per-window, single-use touch for renderer touch
 drags"

This is a reland of commit 049c133a108ec6d213453d53e9c7bdb00aad2ff7

The original change was reverted because webui_toolbar_interactive_uitest
failed to compile on Mac: it included ui/aura/test/env_test_helper.h
unconditionally, which transitively included ui/aura/window.h via
env_input_state_controller.h, and window.h #errors on Mac.

Changes since the original:
- env_input_state_controller.h forward-declares aura::Window instead
  of including ui/aura/window.h. The include moves to the .cc file.
- The ui/aura includes in webui_toolbar_interactive_uitest.cc are now
  guarded by USE_AURA. This landed separately in crrev.com/1724848, so
  it is no longer part of this change.

Original change's description:
> Require a per-window, single-use touch for renderer touch drags
>
> WebContentsViewAura::StartDragging validated a renderer-initiated
> touch drag with aura::Env::is_touch_down(), a process-global flag that
> reports whether any touch is down anywhere. This allowed a compromised
> renderer to start a touch drag that latched onto a touch that was not
> on its web contents, or onto a touch whose release was never observed:
>
> - On Ozone/Wayland, a touch held on the window caption to move the
>   window satisfied the check, and the platform drag started with the
>   user's touch serial.
> - On Ozone/X11, X11WholeScreenMoveLoop consumes the touch release that
>   ends a touch drag, leaving is_touch_down() stuck true. The renderer
>   could then repeatedly start drags with no touch down, each grabbing
>   the pointer and turning the user's next tap or click anywhere on the
>   desktop into a drop of attacker-controlled data.
>
> EnvInputStateController now also tracks, for each active touch, the
> window its press was dispatched to, its most recent screen location, and
> whether it has already been used to start a drag. Non-client presses
> (which may become window moves) are not associated with their target. A
> new Env::ConsumeTouchForDrag(window) returns the location of an unused
> touch pressed on `window` and marks it as used. StartDragging uses it
> for touch drags, so the touch must have been pressed on the web contents
> itself, each press can start at most one drag, and the drag starts at
> the browser-observed touch location rather than falling back to the
> renderer-supplied one. The existing is_touch_down() semantics are
> unchanged.
>
